   "text": "I read the drawing as a cyclic peptide with a free N-terminus and a C-terminal primary amide. I assumed L-amino-acid stereochemistry throughout and checked only the 4-chloro-Phe centre against the wedges. I did not check the other centres against their wedges or hashes, and the Ser centre could be D.\n\n**Residues, from the N-terminus:**\n1. 4-Chloro-Phe, with a free NH2.\n2. Dap (2,3-diaminopropionic acid), whose side-chain NH is acylated by the Asp side chain, forming the macrocycle.\n3. Tyr.\n4. Phe carrying a para phenylurea, written as 4-(NH\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH\u2013Ph).\n5. Lys, with a free (CH2)4NH2 side chain.\n6. Ser, with a CH2OH side chain.\n7. Asp, linked through its side-chain carbonyl to the Dap nitrogen.\n8. Tyr-NH2 at the C-terminus.\n\n**Stereochemistry check:** For the 4-chloro-Phe \u03b1-carbon, the NH2 is hashed (pointing away) and the H is toward the viewer. Reading N \u2192 C(=O) \u2192 CH2 gives a clockwise path on screen, so the true order is reversed, which gives S, the L configuration. I then wrote the SMILES with L configuration at every residue, adjusting the @/@@ symbols for each atom's neighbour order.\n\nSMILES: N[C@@H](Cc1ccc(Cl)cc1)C(=O)N[C@H]2CNC(=O)C[C@@H](C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc3ccc(O)cc3)C(N)=O)NC(=O)[C@H](CO)NC(=O)[C@H](CCCCN)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc4ccc(NC(=O)Nc5ccccc5)cc4)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc6ccc(O)cc6)NC2=O"
